WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.420
Modern string security is not only about buffer safety.

2
00:00:04.520 --> 00:00:06.600
Encoding determines what bytes mean.

3
00:00:06.700 --> 00:00:18.220
If one component treats data as UTF-8, another treats it as a local code page, and another treats it as arbitrary bytes, comparisons and validation can become inconsistent.

4
00:00:18.500 --> 00:00:20.420
Unicode adds another layer.

5
00:00:20.520 --> 00:00:25.320
Text that appears similar to a person may have different underlying representations.

6
00:00:25.420 --> 00:00:29.340
Some characters can be represented in composed or decomposed forms.

7
00:00:29.440 --> 00:00:33.200
Some scripts contain characters that look alike but are not the same.

8
00:00:33.300 --> 00:00:40.820
These facts matter when strings are used as identifiers, user names, file names, policy values, or security labels.

9
00:00:41.100 --> 00:00:47.340
Normalization can make comparisons more predictable by converting text into a chosen canonical form.

10
00:00:47.440 --> 00:00:51.200
That does not mean every string should always be normalized the same way.

11
00:00:51.300 --> 00:00:58.980
It means the application should define which fields require canonical comparison and which form is expected before the comparison happens.

12
00:00:59.260 --> 00:01:02.300
Case conversion and locale also need attention.

13
00:01:02.400 --> 00:01:08.400
Rules that seem obvious in simple ASCII examples may not hold for all languages or platforms.

14
00:01:08.500 --> 00:01:15.780
Security-sensitive comparison should avoid accidental dependence on the current process locale unless that is part of the design.

15
00:01:16.060 --> 00:01:27.340
The practical guidance is to choose expected encodings, validate conversions, normalize before security-sensitive comparison where appropriate, and document the canonical form.

16
00:01:27.440 --> 00:01:31.200
Avoid making security decisions on inconsistent representations.
